---
/**
 * Ohio's 99 House districts, with school funding apportioned across them.
 *
 * Ordered by apportioned state aid, because the question a legislative view is for is scale: how
 * much of Ohio's school money flows through the districts a given member represents.
 */
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { loadFeed } from "../lib/feed.ts";
import { count, money, pct } from "../lib/format.ts";
import * as routes from "../lib/routes.ts";

const { bundle } = loadFeed();
const ranked = [...bundle.house_districts].sort((a, b) => b.realized_aid - a.realized_aid);
const total = ranked.reduce((a, h) => a + h.realized_aid, 0);
const split = bundle.districts.filter((d) => d.house_districts.length > 1).length;
const widest = [...bundle.districts].sort(
  (a, b) => b.house_districts.length - a.house_districts.length,
)[0]!;
---

<Base
  title="House districts"
  description={`Ohio's 99 House districts, with school funding apportioned across them from census blocks — an estimate, since no House district is a unit of account in Ohio's funding system.`}
  section="house"
  wide
>
  <h1>House districts</h1>
  <p class="sub">
    Ohio's {count(ranked.length)} House districts, ordered by the state aid flowing through the
    schools in each. {count(split)} of {count(bundle.districts.length)} school districts straddle
    two or more of them; {widest.name} spans {count(widest.house_districts.length)}.
  </p>

  <div class="card">
    <h2>These figures are estimates, and nobody publishes them</h2>
    <p class="note">
      The department computes funding <strong>per school district</strong> and stops. No House
      district is a unit of account anywhere in Ohio's funding system, and most school districts
      cross House district lines — so every figure on these pages is <em>derived</em>, by splitting
      each school district across the House districts it overlaps in proportion to
      <strong>under-18 population from the 2020 census</strong>.
    </p>
    <p class="note">
      One thing about them is exact: each school district's shares sum to one, so these 99 rows add
      to {money(total)}, the statewide total, to the cent. Everything else is an estimate. Use them
      for scale and comparison; for what a district is actually entitled to, go to that district's
      own page, where the figure is the department's.
    </p>
    <p class="note">
      Two caveats worth carrying. Under-18 population is a proxy for pupils — it counts children in
      community schools, private schools and none. And it is a 2020 count applied to a FY{bundle.fiscal_year}
      model, so a House district that has grown or emptied since the census is weighted as it was.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="card">
    <div class="scroll"><table>
      <thead><tr>
        <th>District</th><th class="tnum">State aid</th><th class="tnum">Share of Ohio</th>
        <th class="tnum">Pupils</th><th class="tnum">Guarantee</th>
        <th class="tnum">Schools</th><th class="tnum">Whole</th>
      </tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        {ranked.map((h) => (
          <tr>
            <th><a href={routes.houseDistrict(h.number)}>House {Number(h.number)}</a></th>
            <td class="tnum">{money(h.realized_aid)}</td>
            <td class="tnum n">{pct(h.realized_aid / total, 2)}</td>
            <td class="tnum">{count(Math.round(h.adm))}</td>
            <td class="tnum">{h.guarantee <= 0 ? "—" : money(h.guarantee)}</td>
            <td class="tnum">{count(h.members.length)}</td>
            <td class="tnum n">{count(h.districts_wholly_inside)}</td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table></div>
    <p class="note">
      "Schools" counts the school districts with population in the House district, including
      slivers; "whole" counts those lying entirely inside it. A House district where the two are
      equal contains its schools completely, and a member there speaks for every pupil in them.
    </p>
  </div>
</Base>
